\chapter{Cancellation and monotonic deadlines}
\label{ch:44}
\index{Cancellation and monotonic deadlines}
\index{Cancellation!deadlines}

Internal fiber cancellation consumes the handle. Before dispatch it can prevent the body from starting. For running or blocked children it uses cooperative interruption, withdraws waits, drains checked deferred and lexical cleanup, and accounts for descendants before returning. A well-typed program still needs an execution path that cooperates with cancellation. Cleanup observations can distinguish a cancelled wait from normal return.

A \OnxCodeTerm{poll after} arm gives a timed choice that the program can handle. A \OnxCodeTerm{taskgroup within} deadline has a different contract in this release. Expiry cooperatively drains the timed group and terminates the run with a located diagnostic and exit status 73. Design a regression test that blocks a child and records its cleanup, then check the host-visible result. Keep a separate no-expiry case that consumes all handles normally. Neither deadline form implements general async/await.

\section{Use a handshake to locate cleanup}
In the cancellation example, the child registers a deferred print of 90 before it sends a notification. The parent waits to receive the notification before requesting cancellation. This handshake answers a question that elapsed time cannot: has the child actually reached the registration? A delay followed by a cancellation would not by itself establish that fact. The notification has already been transferred; observing cancellation after a communication cannot undo a transfer the other side has received.

The child then has a loop with generated cancellation points. For a request made before completion, the parent consumes the handle and waits for the child's cleanup; printing 90 is part of that cleanup, not a result obtained by joining the cancelled handle. If instead a child has already completed, cancellation consumes its handle but reports false. A test of cancellation before dispatch has a third expectation: a defer in a body that never started was never registered.

Compare the two subsequent timer choices. A timer-only poll with a five millisecond duration can eventually produce 7. A poll offering a one second timer and a default can take the default immediately when nothing else is ready; the mere presence of a timer does not require waiting one second. Changing the enclosing group's limit to zero has quite another effect: expiry occurs before its first body statement. No child marker should then be used as evidence that cleanup was skipped.

A nested group with a longer limit cannot extend its enclosing deadline. Calculate both absolute expiry times before deciding which location a timeout diagnostic should name.

